From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuya Hamamachi <yuya.hamamachi.sx@renesas.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] hw/arm/xen_arm: create virtio-mmio devices only without PCIe

xen_arm_init() unconditionally instantiates NR_VIRTIO_MMIO_DEVICES (10)
virtio-mmio devices starting at GUEST_VIRTIO_MMIO_BASE (0x02000000) with
IRQs GUEST_VIRTIO_MMIO_SPI_FIRST..LAST (33..42).

Every such device region is registered to Qemu's IOREQ server by
xen_set_memory(), because it is not ram_memory. As a result Qemu claims
the whole 0x02000000..0x020013ff range even when it only serves
virtio-pci devices.

This collides with standalone virtio-mmio backends (e.g. virtio-disk),
which libxl also places at GUEST_VIRTIO_MMIO_BASE with IRQ 33. Xen's
ioreq_server_select() then routes the guest's MMIO accesses to Qemu
instead of the standalone backend. Qemu's virtio-mmio device has no
backend attached, so it reports device_id 0 and the guest's
virtio_mmio_probe() silently fails with -ENODEV, treating the device as
a dummy placeholder.

The DPRINTF below the PCIe setup already states the intended behaviour
("PCIe host bridge is not available, only virtio-mmio can be used"), so
make the two transports mutually exclusive: only create the virtio-mmio
devices when no PCIe host bridge is available. Configurations that do
not use virtio-pci keep the previous behaviour.

Signed-off-by: Yuya Hamamachi <yuya.hamamachi.sx@renesas.com>
---
 hw/arm/xen_arm.c | 7 ++++---
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/hw/arm/xen_arm.c b/hw/arm/xen_arm.c
--- a/hw/arm/xen_arm.c
+++ b/hw/arm/xen_arm.c
@@ -383,11 +383,12 @@
     }
 
     xen_init_ram(machine);
-    xen_create_virtio_mmio_devices(xam);
-    if (!xen_get_pcie_params(xam))
+    if (!xen_get_pcie_params(xam)) {
         xen_create_pcie(xam);
-    else
+    } else {
         DPRINTF("PCIe host bridge is not available, only virtio-mmio can be used\n");
+        xen_create_virtio_mmio_devices(xam);
+    }
 
     xen_enable_tpm();
 
